const e={note:`Read this hall knowing that its people left no books. Almost everything in it is reconstructed from inscriptions — dedications, curses, tomb-lids, temple tariffs — which are formulaic by design and say what the formula requires rather than what a modern reader wants; and from Greek and Latin writers describing commercial rivals, and Hebrew and Christian writers describing an error. Where a Phoenician text and an outside report disagree, both are given, and neither is treated as neutral. Three further cautions. "Phoenician" is a Greek word, not a Phoenician one: there was never a Phoenician state, and Josephine Quinn has argued at book length that there was no Phoenician self-identity either — the people of Byblos, Sidon, Tyre and Carthage identified by city, and the term is used here as a convenience of scholarship, which is all it is. Dating rests largely on palaeography, a relative method with few fixed points, so the numbers move: the Ahiram sarcophagus is given below at both the thirteenth century of its excavator's tomb-group and the tenth or ninth of its letter-forms, and the reader should expect a range rather than a date. And the script writes no vowels, so every vocalization printed here — Melqart, Eshmun, Tanit, Ahiram — is a convention drawn from Greek and Latin transcriptions and from cognate languages, not something the inscriptions record. The tophet current ends without a verdict, because the field has not reached one.`,themes:[{slug:"twenty-two",title:"The Twenty-Two",native:"𐤀𐤋𐤐 𐤁𐤕",translit:"ʾalp bet",summary:"Twenty-two signs, one for each consonant, learnable in an afternoon — and the ancestor of nearly every alphabet now in use, including the one this sentence is printed in.",paragraphs:["The Phoenician script has twenty-two letters and no more. Each stands for a consonant; none stands for a vowel; there are no determinatives, no logograms, no syllabic values, no signs whose function is to tell the reader how to read the other signs. Set beside the writing systems it grew up among, the economy is startling. Mesopotamian cuneiform in its Old Babylonian form required control of several hundred signs with multiple readings each; Egyptian used a comparable inventory across hieroglyphic and hieratic; the alphabetic cuneiform of Ugarit, itself an alphabet, used thirty. The Phoenician twenty-two could be taught to a merchant's son in an afternoon and written with a stylus, a brush or a chisel with equal ease. That combination — a very small closed set, no professional apprenticeship, no dependence on a particular medium — is what carried it, and it carried it not through conquest but through commerce, which is the unusual part of the story.","Its ancestry runs back to the early alphabetic inscriptions of the Sinai and Egypt. Flinders Petrie found the Serabit el-Khadim material at the turquoise mines in 1905; Alan Gardiner in 1916 identified a recurring sequence as lbʿlt, 'to the Lady', and that single reading is the hinge on which the decipherment of the earliest alphabet turns. Two further inscriptions were found in the 1990s at Wadi el-Hol in the Egyptian western desert by John and Deborah Darnell, who date them to the early second millennium BCE; others place them later, and the question is open. The principle is acrophony: a sign drawn as an object stands for the first sound of that object's name. A head stands for r because the word for head begins with r. Whether the Phoenician letters were still felt as pictures by the time they were standardised around the eleventh century BCE is doubtful — most had already been abstracted into strokes — but the names outlived the pictures, and it is the names that travelled.","Here the hall must be honest about a gap. No Phoenician text lists the letters' names. The names conventionally used — ʾalp, bet, gaml, delt, he, wau, zai, ḥet, ṭet, yod, kaf, lamd, mem, nun, semk, ʿain, pe, ṣade, qof, roš, šin, tau — are reconstructions built from the Hebrew names recorded in the Masoretic tradition, from the Greek names alpha, bēta, gamma, which are meaningless in Greek and therefore preserved from elsewhere, and from later Semitic alphabet-lists. Several meanings are secure and famous: ʾalp an ox, bet a house, delt a door, yod a hand, mem water, ʿain an eye, roš a head, tau a mark. Several are not. What gaml means is argued between camel and throwstick; he, ṭet, semk, ṣade and qof are frankly obscure, and the standard glosses for them — jubilation, wheel, fish or pillar, fish-hook, monkey — are guesses of varying age and confidence. The Hebrew hall teaches these names as Hebrew, which they became; they are Canaanite first, and the Phoenician forms are the earlier attested shapes.","The order is older than any Phoenician inscription and has proved almost unbreakable. Cuneiform abecedaries from Ras Shamra of the thirteenth century BCE run the thirty Ugaritic signs in a sequence whose skeleton is the sequence still recited today; a second order, the halaḥam, is also attested at Ugarit and survives in Ancient South Arabian and in Geʿez, so the two were current side by side and one of them simply won. The Izbet Sartah ostracon from the central hill country, generally dated between about 1200 and 1000 BCE, carries an abecedary of twenty-two letters written left to right by a hand that was evidently practising. Direction had not yet settled: early alphabetic writing runs in either direction and sometimes boustrophedon, and Phoenician fixed on right-to-left while Greek, after a period of both, fixed on left-to-right. What did not change was the order, which passed through Greek into Latin and reaches the reader as ABC.","One negative fact deserves its own sentence, because it is the opposite of what a reader coming from the Hebrew or Greek halls will expect. The Phoenicians did not use their letters as numbers. Their numerals are separate signs — vertical strokes for units, distinct marks for ten, twenty and a hundred, encoded in Unicode alongside the letters — and there is no evidence at all of a Phoenician practice of summing words. Isopsephy is a Greek development, gematria a Jewish one, and both required a step the Phoenicians never took. The alphabet that made every later letter-mysticism possible carried none of its own. The people who invented it did not think of themselves as Phoenicians either: the word is Greek, from phoinix, and Augustine, writing at Hippo in the early fifth century CE, reports that when the country people around him were asked what they were they answered in Punic that they were Chanani — Canaanites, knʿn, which is the name the inscriptions use."],terms:[{native:"𐤀𐤋𐤐",translit:"ʾlp",gloss:"ox, and also 'thousand'; the reconstructed name of the first letter, whose ox-head turned a quarter-circle and then upside down becomes the capital A"},{native:"𐤓𐤀𐤔",translit:"rʾš",gloss:"head; the letter-name behind Greek rho and Latin R, and an ordinary Phoenician noun for a chief or a beginning"},{native:"𐤏𐤍",translit:"ʿn",gloss:"eye; the pharyngeal consonant Greek could not pronounce, which is why the eye became the letter O"},{native:"𐤉𐤃",translit:"yd",gloss:"hand; the smallest letter in every descendant script, and the source of both I and J"},{native:"𐤊𐤕𐤁",translit:"ktb",gloss:"to write, to inscribe; the root that gives Hebrew ketav, Aramaic ketab and Arabic kataba, and the ordinary verb of the inscriptions"},{native:"𐤀𐤁𐤍",translit:"ʾbn",gloss:"stone; the medium on which almost the whole surviving corpus is written, and the reason there is a corpus at all"}],texts:[{title:"The Serabit el-Khadim inscriptions (Sinai)",note:"Found by Flinders Petrie in 1905 at the turquoise mines; Gardiner's 1916 reading of lbʿlt, 'to the Lady', opened the earliest alphabet to reading. Dated to the first half of the second millennium BCE, with the range still argued."},{title:"The Wadi el-Hol inscriptions",note:"Two short alphabetic texts in the Egyptian western desert, published by John and Deborah Darnell, who place them around 1900–1800 BCE; other scholars date them lower. The earliest alphabetic writing yet found in Egypt proper."},{title:"The Ugaritic abecedaries (Ras Shamra)",note:"Thirteenth-century BCE cuneiform tablets listing the thirty alphabetic signs in order — the earliest evidence that the sequence itself is a transmitted object, and evidence that two rival orders existed."},{title:"The Izbet Sartah ostracon",note:"Five lines scratched on a sherd, the last a twenty-two-letter abecedary running left to right; c. 1200–1000 BCE, and apparently a schoolroom exercise."},{title:"H. Donner and W. Röllig, Kanaanäische und aramäische Inschriften",note:"Cited throughout this hall as KAI; with the older Corpus Inscriptionum Semiticarum Pars I, the standard edition in which the Phoenician and Punic corpus can actually be consulted rather than described."}]},{slug:"ahiram",title:"The Coffin of Ahiram",native:"𐤀𐤓𐤍",translit:"ʾarōn",summary:"The earliest long text in the alphabet is a threat carved on the rim of a sarcophagus lid, and nobody agrees when it was cut.",paragraphs:["In 1923 Pierre Montet, excavating the royal necropolis at Byblos, opened shaft tomb V and found a limestone sarcophagus with lions carved at its corners and a banquet scene on its side. Around the rim of the lid ran a single line of alphabetic writing, and a second line on the box: a coffin which Ittobaal son of Ahiram, king of Byblos, made for Ahiram his father, when he set him in eternity. Then the sentence that makes the object famous. If any king among kings, or governor among governors, or army commander should come up against Byblos and uncover this coffin — may the sceptre of his rule be stripped away, may the throne of his kingship be overturned, and may peace flee from Byblos. What survives as the earliest substantial composition in the ancestor of the Latin alphabet is therefore not a hymn, a chronicle or a contract. It is a curse, and its purpose is to make the writing itself do work after the writer is dead.","The vocabulary rewards attention. The word for the coffin is ʾrn, the same word as the ark of the Hebrew Bible; the sceptre is ḥṭr and the rule it belongs to is mšpṭ, from the root that gives the Carthaginian magistrates their title of suffete; the throne is ksʾ. The verb that governs the whole opening is pʿl, made — the standard Phoenician formula for a dedicated object, which will be met again on every votive stele in this hall. Down the tomb shaft a second, cruder text was scratched, warning whoever descends. Its reading is disputed at almost every word, and the more careful editors print it with brackets. That two texts of such different registers sit in one tomb is itself informative: monumental Phoenician and scratched Phoenician were both current, and only the monumental kind normally survives.","The dating is the argument. Montet's tomb group contained Egyptian material of the thirteenth century BCE, including an alabaster vessel with the cartouche of Ramesses II, and on that association the burial was first assigned to the thirteenth century — which would make the alphabet's monumental use two centuries older than anyone had supposed. The palaeographic argument runs the other way. The letter forms belong with the tenth-century Byblian royal inscriptions, and W. F. Albright placed the text around 1000 BCE, taking the tomb to have been cut earlier and reused; this is the majority position and has been for decades. It is not unanimous. Reinhard Lehmann's full re-edition of the sarcophagus and shaft inscriptions argues for a later, ninth-century placement on epigraphic grounds, and the relationship of the Ahiram text to the other Byblian royal inscriptions — Yeḥimilk, Abibaal, Elibaal, Shipitbaal — is not settled either, since their relative sequence has itself been reordered more than once. The honest statement is that the inscription belongs somewhere between the thirteenth century of its excavator's tomb-group and the ninth of its most recent editor, with the tenth as the field's centre of gravity.","Ahiram is the longest of a scatter of very early alphabetic texts that share a habit: they are short, they name an owner, and several of them threaten. Five bronze arrowheads from el-Khadr near Bethlehem, dated to the eleventh century BCE, carry nothing but 'arrow of' followed by a personal name — the alphabet used to mark a weapon, at a period when almost nothing else is written in it. The Nora Stone from Sardinia, generally placed in the ninth century, is the earliest Phoenician inscription in the western Mediterranean and contains a word read by many as Tarshish, which would make it a record of a voyage; the reading is contested and so is nearly every clause of the text. What these have in common is that they are objects with writing on them rather than documents. The alphabet was portable because it was used on portable things.","The curse formula is the genre's real contribution to the history of religion, and it recurs for a thousand years. The Sidonian sarcophagi of Tabnit and Eshmunazar, treated in a later current, both open by telling the reader not to open them and end by promising the intruder no resting place among the shades and no seed under the sun. What is being invoked is not a spell in any technical sense; there is no procedure, no material, no operator. It is a sentence deposited in stone and left to act, addressed to a literate future stranger, and its efficacy depends entirely on the reader's belief that the gods named will read over his shoulder. That is a specific and rather austere theory of what writing is for, and it is the first thing this alphabet was asked to do at length."],terms:[{native:"𐤀𐤓𐤍",translit:"ʾrn",gloss:"chest, coffin, sarcophagus; the first word of the inscription and cognate with the biblical aron, the ark"},{native:"𐤀𐤇𐤓𐤌",translit:"ʾḥrm",gloss:"Ahiram, king of Byblos; the vocalization is conventional, since the script writes no vowels"},{native:"𐤇𐤈𐤓",translit:"ḥṭr",gloss:"staff, sceptre; in the curse it is the sceptre of his mšpṭ, his rule — the same root as the suffete, the Carthaginian chief magistrate"},{native:"𐤊𐤎𐤀",translit:"ksʾ",gloss:"throne; what the curse asks to see overturned, paired with the sceptre stripped away"},{native:"𐤏𐤋𐤌",translit:"ʿlm",gloss:"eternity, the remote time; the phrase 'when he set him in eternity' is read by some as a euphemism for burial and by others as naming the tomb itself"},{native:"𐤐𐤏𐤋",translit:"pʿl",gloss:"he made; the standard verb of the dedication formula across a thousand years of Phoenician and Punic inscriptions"}],texts:[{title:"The Ahiram sarcophagus inscription (KAI 1)",note:"Byblos, shaft tomb V, excavated by Montet in 1923. Two lines on lid and box: a dedication by the son and a curse against anyone who opens the coffin."},{title:"The Byblos shaft graffito",note:"Scratched on the wall of the same tomb's descent; badly damaged and read differently by every editor, which is itself a useful corrective to how confidently short texts are usually quoted."},{title:"The Byblian royal inscriptions: Yeḥimilk (KAI 4), Abibaal (KAI 5), Elibaal (KAI 6), Shipitbaal (KAI 7)",note:"The tenth-century series against which Ahiram is dated palaeographically; Abibaal and Elibaal are cut on reused Egyptian statues of Sheshonq I and Osorkon I, which supplies rare external anchors."},{title:"The el-Khadr arrowheads",note:"Eleventh-century BCE inscribed bronze points from near Bethlehem, reading only 'arrow of' plus a name; among the oldest alphabetic texts in the Levant and evidence for ordinary, non-monumental use."},{title:"The Nora Stone (KAI 46)",note:"Sardinia, ninth century BCE by the usual reckoning; the earliest Phoenician inscription in the west. Almost every word of its reading, including the possible mention of Tarshish, is disputed."}]},{slug:"the-vowel",title:"The Invention of the Vowel",native:"𐤀 𐤄 𐤅 𐤉 𐤏",translit:"ʾ h w y ʿ",summary:"Greek took twenty-two letters it could not entirely use, and turned the ones it could not pronounce into the sounds no alphabet had ever written.",paragraphs:["A consonantal alphabet suits a Semitic language, where the root is a skeleton of consonants and the vowels inflect it; a reader who knows the language supplies them. Greek is not built that way. Its vowels carry lexical weight, its verbs and cases turn on them, and a Greek text written in bare consonants is close to unreadable even to a Greek. So when Greeks took the Phoenician letters, somewhere between the eleventh and eighth centuries BCE, they faced a problem the lenders did not have — and solved it with the borrowed material rather than by inventing new signs. Phoenician had consonants Greek lacked: the glottal stop of ʾalp, the h of he, the pharyngeal fricative of ḥet, the deeper pharyngeal of ʿain. A Greek hearing a Phoenician recite the alphabet would have heard those letters as beginning with a vowel, because to a Greek ear that is all that was there. Those letters became alpha, epsilon, eta and omicron. Waw and yod, already semi-vowels, became upsilon and iota. Nothing was invented; five or six sounds were reassigned, and reading changed permanently.","The Greek names are the fingerprint of the loan. Alpha, bēta, gamma, delta mean nothing whatever in Greek. In a Canaanite language they mean ox, house, camel-or-throwstick, door. Herodotus knew this. At Histories 5.58 he says that the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought many kinds of learning to the Greeks and among them grammata, which the Greeks had not had before, and that the Ionians of his own day still called letters phoinikeia, Phoenician things. He is reporting a tradition, not conducting an inquiry, and his Cadmus is a mythological figure; but the linguistic evidence he cites in passing — a live word for 'letters' meaning 'the Phoenician stuff' — is worth more than the myth around it.","When it happened is contested and the parties are clear. The earliest Greek inscriptions that survive are eighth-century: the Dipylon oinochoe from Athens of about 740 BCE, and the cup from Pithekoussai on Ischia inscribed a few decades later with three lines mentioning Nestor. Barry Powell has argued that the adoption was a single deliberate act around 800 BCE by an individual adapter whose purpose was to record hexameter verse, and specifically Homer. Joseph Naveh argued from letter-forms for a much earlier transfer, in the twelfth or eleventh century, on the grounds that the Greek shapes match Phoenician shapes of that period rather than of the eighth; Frank Moore Cross argued similarly from the Semitist's side. The counter-argument is the four-century silence in the Greek material, which Naveh attributed to the perishability of what was written on. Whether the vowels were a deliberate invention or an accident of mishearing is a second and separate dispute: the deliberate model needs a single adapter, while the alternative holds that Greek learners simply misheard the gutturals and that the system settled afterwards.","The residue of the loan is visible in the Greek alphabet forever after. Letters Greek did not need were kept anyway, in place, as numerals: digamma from waw holds the value 6, koppa from qof holds 90, and the sign called sampi holds 900 — values which make sense only if the letters are counted in the Phoenician order with the dead letters still standing in it. The Greek number system is a fossil of a Phoenician alphabet, and the isopsephy of the Greek hall runs on it. What Greek added, it added at the end, after tau: upsilon, phi, chi, psi and finally omega, appended where a borrowed alphabet has room. And the aspirate ḥet had a divided career — in the psilotic East Ionic dialects, which had lost initial h, the letter was free to become long ē, while elsewhere it stayed the rough breathing, which is why Latin H is a consonant and Greek eta is a vowel.","From Greek the alphabet went west twice over. Etruscan took it in the eighth or seventh century BCE, keeping letters it had no use for: the ivory writing tablet from Marsiliana d'Albegna, of about 700 BCE, carries a model alphabet of twenty-six letters in a border, including signs Etruscan never wrote, which shows an alphabet being transmitted as a memorised object rather than as a working inventory. Latin took it from Etruscan, receiving C from gamma and later manufacturing G by adding a stroke — a change Roman tradition credits to Spurius Carvilius Ruga in the third century BCE — and receiving F from the old waw. Eastward the Phoenician letters went to Paleo-Hebrew directly, and to Aramaic, and from Aramaic to Syriac, to Arabic, to the Sogdian and Mongolian scripts; whether Brahmi and so the Indic scripts descend from Aramaic, as Georg Bühler argued and many still hold, or arose independently, as an Indian school has long maintained, is unresolved. The twenty-two letters on the rim of Ahiram's coffin are, on any of these accounts, the most consequential twenty-two objects in the history of writing."],terms:[{native:"𐤀",translit:"ʾ (ʾalp)",gloss:"the glottal stop; Greek had no such consonant, heard the letter-name as beginning with a vowel, and made it alpha"},{native:"𐤄",translit:"h (he)",gloss:"a plain h; reassigned by Greek to the short e of epsilon, whose later name means simply 'bare e'"},{native:"𐤇",translit:"ḥ (ḥet)",gloss:"a voiceless pharyngeal fricative; kept as the aspirate in some Greek dialects and freed to become the long vowel eta in those that had lost initial h — the ancestor of both H and E-long"},{native:"𐤅",translit:"w (wau)",gloss:"w; became upsilon, and its abandoned form was retained in place as the numeral 6, digamma — the clearest fossil of the borrowing"},{native:"𐤉",translit:"y (yod)",gloss:"y; became iota, and through Latin both I and J"},{native:"𐤏",translit:"ʿ (ʿain)",gloss:"a voiced pharyngeal; the letter drawn as an eye, which Greek could not pronounce and turned into omicron"}],texts:[{title:"Herodotus, Histories 5.58–61",note:"The Cadmus tradition, and the report that Ionians still called letters phoinikeia — a live usage that outweighs the myth attached to it."},{title:"The Dipylon oinochoe and the 'Cup of Nestor' (Pithekoussai)",note:"Athens, c. 740 BCE, and Ischia, c. 730–720 BCE; the earliest substantial Greek alphabetic writing, and both of them verse. The second is the evidence Powell's argument leans on."},{title:"Barry B. Powell, Homer and the Origin of the Greek Alphabet (1991)",note:"The case for a single adapter around 800 BCE working to record hexameter; the strongest statement of the late, deliberate model."},{title:"Joseph Naveh, Early History of the Alphabet (1982)",note:"The case for transfer in the twelfth or eleventh century on palaeographic grounds, against the four-century gap in surviving Greek material."},{title:"The Marsiliana d'Albegna abecedarium",note:"An ivory writing tablet of c. 700 BCE with a twenty-six-letter model alphabet in its border, including letters Etruscan never used — an alphabet transmitted whole, before being pruned."}]},{slug:"byblos",title:"Byblos and the Book",native:"𐤂𐤁𐤋",translit:"Gubl",summary:"The English word Bible is the name of a Phoenician port, and the reason no Phoenician books survive is the commodity that port sold.",paragraphs:["The city its own inhabitants called Gebal, gbl, was known to Greeks as Byblos, and byblos in Greek is the papyrus plant and the writing material made from it; from that comes biblion, a roll or book, and from the plural ta biblia, the books, comes the Bible. The usual reconstruction has the material named after the port through which Egyptian papyrus reached the Aegean, and this remains the standard view; a minority has argued the transfer ran the other way or through a lost intermediate, and the semantics have never been proved. Either way the coincidence is exact and instructive. The oldest continuously occupied city on the Phoenician coast, whose trade with Egypt is attested from the third millennium BCE, gave its name to the physical form of the book — and left almost no books, because papyrus does not survive in a wet coastal climate. What Egypt's desert preserved by the ton, Lebanon's rainfall destroyed entirely. The absence at the centre of this hall is in the first instance a fact about humidity.","One Egyptian papyrus preserves a scene at Byblos with a vividness nothing Phoenician can match. The Report of Wenamun, on a roll now in Moscow and generally dated around 1075 BCE, tells of a Theban temple official sent to buy cedar for the barque of Amun. He is robbed on the way, arrives without credentials, and is left waiting in the harbour for weeks by the prince Zakarbaal, who points out with some sharpness that his ancestors were paid properly. Then, during a sacrifice, a young man of the prince's own retinue is seized by the god, falls into ecstasy and delivers an oracle commanding that the Egyptian envoy and his god be received. The prince complies. Whatever the text is — official report, literary composition, or an official report shaped into literature, and scholars have argued all three — it contains the earliest description of ecstatic prophecy on the Levantine coast, several centuries before the Hebrew prophetic books, and it is preserved in Egyptian because the Byblian side wrote on the commodity it was selling.","Byblos also holds the one genuinely undeciphered Phoenician-coast writing system. Maurice Dunand's excavations recovered ten documents — bronze tablets, bronze spatulas and stone fragments — in a script of about a hundred distinct signs, too many for an alphabet and too few for a logographic system, therefore presumed a syllabary. Dunand published them in 1945 as Byblia Grammata, and they have resisted every attempt since. Proposed decipherments, including Édouard Dhorme's and later ones by Brian Colless and by Jan Best, have each claimed a Semitic language behind the signs and none has been generally accepted. The dating is equally unresolved, with proposals running across most of the second millennium BCE. It is called pseudo-hieroglyphic because some signs resemble Egyptian ones; the name is a description of appearance and commits its users to nothing.","The city's own goddess is the Baalat Gebal, bʿlt gbl, the Lady of Byblos, whose temple is attested from the third millennium and whom Egyptians identified with Hathor — the identification runs so deep that Egyptian dedications stand in her sanctuary and her iconography borrows the Hathoric wig and horns. She is named in the earliest of the Byblian royal inscriptions, that of Yeḥimilk, which invokes bʿl šmm, the Lord of Heaven, together with the Lady of Byblos and mpḥrt ʾl gbl qdšm, the assembly of the holy gods of Byblos. That last phrase is worth pausing on: it is a Phoenician text naming a divine council as a collective, in a formula built on the same root, qdš, that runs through the whole Northwest Semitic vocabulary of holiness. It is one of a very small number of places where a Phoenician inscription tells us something about the structure of the pantheon rather than merely naming a recipient.","What Byblos supplies to this hall, then, is the material explanation of its own poverty. Kings' names on stone; a lady's temple; a syllabary nobody can read; and, in an Egyptian file copy, one afternoon in a harbour when a god spoke through a boy. Everything written for use — the accounts of the timber trade, the letters between courts, whatever mythology stood behind the divine assembly of Yeḥimilk's inscription — was written on rolls that are gone. When Philo of Byblos, a millennium and a half later, claimed to be translating an ancient Phoenician priest, the claim was at least plausible in a way it would not have been for another city, because Byblos really had been in the business of writing. Whether he was translating anything is the subject of a later current in this hall."],terms:[{native:"𐤂𐤁𐤋",translit:"gbl",gloss:"Gebal, the city Greeks called Byblos; the root suggests a boundary or a mountain, and the modern town of Jbeil keeps the name"},{native:"𐤁𐤏𐤋𐤕 𐤂𐤁𐤋",translit:"bʿlt gbl",gloss:"the Lady of Byblos, the city goddess; the feminine of the ordinary word for lord, and identified by Egyptians with Hathor"},{native:"𐤀𐤃𐤍",translit:"ʾdn",gloss:"lord, master; used of gods, kings and the addressee of a letter, and the source of the Greek name Adonis"},{native:"𐤉𐤇𐤌𐤋𐤊",translit:"yḥmlk",gloss:"Yeḥimilk, 'may the king live'; a Byblian king whose inscription names the Lord of Heaven, the Lady of Byblos and the assembly of the holy gods"},{native:"𐤆𐤊𐤓𐤁𐤏𐤋",translit:"zkrbʿl",gloss:"Zakarbaal, 'Baal has remembered'; the name of the prince who keeps Wenamun waiting, and a Byblian royal name attested epigraphically as well"},{native:"𐤔𐤐𐤈𐤁𐤏𐤋",translit:"špṭbʿl",gloss:"Shipitbaal, 'Baal has judged'; a Byblian king, and the same root that names the Carthaginian suffetes"}],texts:[{title:"The Report of Wenamun (Papyrus Moscow 120)",note:"c. 1075 BCE, in hieratic Egyptian; the Byblos harbour scene and the ecstatic oracle. Its genre — report, tale, or one shaped into the other — is argued."},{title:"The Yeḥimilk inscription (KAI 4)",note:"Byblos, tenth century BCE; the earliest naming of Baal Shamem alongside the Lady of Byblos and the assembly of the holy gods, and one of the few Phoenician texts to describe a pantheon collectively."},{title:"Maurice Dunand, Byblia Grammata (1945)",note:"The edition of the ten pseudo-hieroglyphic documents. Undeciphered; every proposed solution since has claimed a Semitic language and none has been accepted."},{title:"Pliny the Elder, Natural History XIII.68–89",note:"The fullest ancient account of how papyrus was made and graded, and of the trade that carried it — the material without which the Phoenician library would have existed and might have survived elsewhere."},{title:"Lucian, De Dea Syria 6–9",note:"Second century CE, in deliberately archaising Ionic; the Adonis rite at Byblos, the river running red each year, and the mourning. Written by a Syrian Greek and partly, perhaps wholly, in a satirical register — usable but not neutral."}]},{slug:"melqart",title:"Melqart, King of the City",native:"𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕",translit:"Milqart",summary:"Tyre's god is not a name but a job description, and his annual rite was to be woken from the dead.",paragraphs:["The god of Tyre is called Melqart, and the word is transparent: mlk qrt, king of the city. It is a title rather than a name, and no other name for him survives. He is the divine counterpart of the human king, bound to one place — and yet he is the god the Phoenicians exported most successfully, because a colony that carried the king of the city carried the city. Herodotus visited his temple at Tyre and describes it at Histories 2.44: two pillars, one of refined gold and one of a green stone he calls smaragdos which shone at night, and priests who told him the sanctuary had been founded when Tyre was founded, twenty-three hundred years before. He went on to Thasos and found another temple of the same god, founded, he was told, five generations before the Greek Herakles was born, and concluded that there were two Herakleses. What he was actually documenting was the interpretatio: Greeks meeting Melqart called him Herakles, and the identification held for a thousand years.","The rite for which he is best known is the egersis, the awakening. Josephus, quoting the Hellenistic historian Menander of Ephesus at Against Apion 1.119, records that Hiram king of Tyre — Solomon's contemporary in the biblical account — was the first to perform the awakening of Herakles, in the month Peritios. The Phoenician and Punic inscriptions supply the officiant: mqm ʾlm, the raiser or awakener of the god, a priestly title attested at Carthage and elsewhere, often extended by a second phrase whose translation is genuinely uncertain and which is variously rendered as bridegroom or spouse of Astarte. What the rite consisted of is not described in any Phoenician source. Greek and Latin writers mention a pyre, and the death of Herakles on his pyre at Mount Oeta has been read since antiquity as an echo of it; whether the Greek myth borrowed the Tyrian rite, or later observers assimilated the rite to the myth they knew, is exactly the kind of question the evidence cannot settle. The safest statement is that Tyre held an annual festival at which its god was treated as having died and been raised, and that a specific priest existed to do the raising.","The god travelled with the ships. At Gadir — ʾgdr, the walled place, which Romans called Gades and which is Cádiz — a sanctuary of Melqart-Herakles stood on an island at the edge of the known world, with an oracle, a perpetual fire and, according to Strabo and later writers, no images. The Pillars of Herakles took their name from it. Carthage sent a tithe annually to the mother-temple at Tyre, and Diodorus reports that the Carthaginians' neglect of that obligation was blamed for their disasters. Hannibal swore his oath as a boy in a temple of the god, and when Alexander besieged Tyre for seven months in 332 BCE the immediate cause, in Arrian's account, was the Tyrians' refusal to let him sacrifice to Herakles in the island sanctuary — an argument about whose god it was, conducted with siege engines.","One document lets us watch the identification being made in writing. The treaty Hannibal concluded with Philip V of Macedon in 215 BCE, quoted by Polybius at 7.9, opens with a list of divine witnesses in Greek, and behind the Greek the Punic gods can be seen: Zeus, Hera and Apollo; then a triad given as the daimon of the Carthaginians, Herakles and Iolaos; then Ares, Triton and Poseidon. Scholars have long read the Herakles as Melqart, and the daimon of the Carthaginians as Tanit or the city's fortune; the identifications behind the third group are argued. The text is a translated diplomatic instrument, and it is the closest thing to an official Carthaginian statement of a pantheon that survives — in Greek, in a Greek historian, in a treaty with a Greek king.","The two marble cippi from Malta are where this god enters modern scholarship. Each is inscribed twice, in Phoenician and in Greek: a dedication by two brothers, Abdosir and Osirshamar, to their lord Melqart, baʿal of Tyre. In 1758 Jean-Jacques Barthélemy laid the Greek beside the Phoenician before the Académie des Inscriptions and read the Phoenician alphabet — the first decipherment of the script, achieved on a votive text to the king of the city. One cippus is in the Louvre, the other in Malta. It is a fair emblem of the whole hall: the language was recovered not from a book but from a pair of stone objects that two brothers set up because they had made a vow, and it was recovered because somebody had thoughtfully written the same thing in Greek."],terms:[{native:"𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕",translit:"mlqrt",gloss:"Melqart: mlk qrt, king of the city — a title serving as the only known name of Tyre's god, and the clearest case of a Phoenician deity defined by an office"},{native:"𐤌𐤒𐤌 𐤀𐤋𐤌",translit:"mqm ʾlm",gloss:"raiser or awakener of the god; a priestly title attested at Carthage and elsewhere, usually connected with the egersis of Melqart, and frequently extended by a phrase whose translation is disputed"},{native:"𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤑𐤓",translit:"bʿl ṣr",gloss:"lord of Tyre; the epithet inscribed on the Malta cippi, showing a colonial dedication addressed to the god of the mother city"},{native:"𐤑𐤓",translit:"ṣr",gloss:"Tyre, 'the rock'; the island city whose temple Herodotus visited and Alexander besieged"},{native:"𐤀𐤂𐤃𐤓",translit:"ʾgdr",gloss:"Gadir, 'the enclosure' or walled place; the westernmost great sanctuary of Melqart, and the name behind Gades and Cádiz"},{native:"𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋",translit:"ḥnbʿl",gloss:"Hannibal, 'Baal has been gracious'; one of the ordinary theophoric names by which the Punic pantheon is best attested, since every citizen carried a god in his"}],texts:[{title:"Herodotus, Histories 2.44",note:"An eyewitness description of the Tyrian temple — the two pillars, the shining green stone, the priests' claim of a 2300-year foundation — and the reasoning that led him to posit two Herakleses."},{title:"Josephus, Against Apion 1.116–120",note:"Quoting Menander of Ephesus on the Tyrian kings; the source for Hiram's institution of the egersis of Herakles in the month Peritios, and the only ancient statement that names the rite."},{title:"The Cippi of Melqart (KAI 47)",note:"Two second-century BCE bilingual marble cippi from Malta, dedicated to Melqart lord of Tyre. Barthélemy used them in 1758 to decipher the Phoenician alphabet."},{title:"Polybius, Histories 7.9",note:"The Greek text of Hannibal's treaty with Philip V, 215 BCE, with its list of divine witnesses — the nearest surviving thing to an official Carthaginian pantheon, and only in translation."},{title:"Arrian, Anabasis 2.15–24",note:"The siege of Tyre, 332 BCE, beginning with Alexander's demand to sacrifice to Herakles in the island temple and the Tyrians' refusal."}]},{slug:"astarte-eshmun",title:"Astarte and Eshmun",native:"𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕",translit:"ʿAštart",summary:"Sidon's two great deities, a healing sanctuary full of statues of children, and two royal sarcophagi that spend their inscriptions begging not to be opened.",paragraphs:["Astarte — ʿštrt, the Ishtar of Mesopotamia and the Ashtoreth of the Hebrew Bible — is the most widely attested Phoenician deity in the epigraphic record, honoured at Sidon, Tyre, Kition on Cyprus, Carthage, Eryx in Sicily and at the Etruscan port of Pyrgi. Greeks called her Aphrodite and Romans Venus, and the identification is close enough to be useful and loose enough to mislead, since her Levantine profile includes sovereignty and war as well as sexuality. At Sidon she shares the city with Eshmun, ʾšmn, a god of healing whom Greeks identified with Asklepios and whose name has no agreed etymology; the derivations proposed run from a word for oil to the numeral eight, and none commands assent. Together they form the standard Sidonian pair, and the royal inscriptions treat building for them as the principal achievement of a reign.","The Eshmun sanctuary at Bostan esh-Sheikh, a few kilometres north of Sidon beside the Awali river, is the best-preserved Phoenician temple complex anywhere. It is built around water: channels, basins and a spring, arranged so that the sanctuary's function and its plumbing are the same thing. Among its finds is a series of marble statues of children, mostly boys, several holding a bird or a small animal, set up as votives — the standard interpretation is that they were dedicated in thanks for a child's recovery, or in hope of it. Whatever the theology behind them, the sanctuary is a place where people came about their children and left an image. That is worth holding in mind when this hall reaches the tophet, because it is evidence from the same culture and it points nowhere near the direction the classical sources point.","Two Sidonian sarcophagi carry the longest royal texts in Phoenician. Both are reused Egyptian anthropoid coffins, which tells its own story about how the Sidonian court regarded Egypt. That of Tabnit, priest of Astarte and king of Sidon, is inscribed on a coffin originally cut for an Egyptian general named Penptah, and its text is almost entirely an appeal: do not open this, do not disturb me, there is no silver with me and no gold, only I lie here — and if you open it anyway, may you have no seed among the living and no resting place with the shades. His son Eshmunazar II's sarcophagus, found at Sidon in 1855 and now in the Louvre, runs to twenty-two lines in the same register, opening with a curse and going on to record that he and his mother Amoashtart built temples for Astarte and for Eshmun at the spring Ydll, and that the Lord of Kings — the Persian great king — granted them Dor and Joppa. The date is usually put in the fifth century BCE, though a lower fourth-century placement has been argued. Here again, as at Byblos, the longest Phoenician texts we possess are attempts to prevent something from being read in the wrong spirit.","Astarte's western reach is documented by an extraordinary find. In 1964 excavations at Pyrgi, the harbour of the Etruscan city of Caere, produced three inscribed gold sheets: two in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, recording a dedication by Thefarie Velianas, ruler of Caere, to Astarte, identified in the Etruscan text with Uni. They date to about 500 BCE and are the first substantial bilingual evidence for Etruscan. The Phoenician text includes the phrase bym qbr ʾlm, on the day of the burial of the god, which some read as evidence for a dying-and-rising rite of the Melqart type transplanted to Italy and others read as a calendrical formula or a reference to a specific festival day; the phrase remains disputed and no reading has become standard. The tablets nevertheless establish that a Phoenician cult formula could be commissioned by an Etruscan king in a Tyrrhenian port.","One dossier attached to Astarte requires an explicit warning. The Kition tariff from Cyprus, a fifth- or fourth-century BCE account of monthly payments by the temple of Astarte, lists its personnel: builders, bakers, barbers, scribes, singers, and two categories written klbm and grm, dogs and puppies. Some scholars take these literally, as animals kept in the sanctuary or their keepers; others as titles of cult personnel; a long tradition read them as male sacred prostitutes, on the strength of Deuteronomy 23:19, which sets the wages of a dog beside those of a prostitute. That last reading now sits inside a broader argument. The classical claim that Near Eastern goddesses were served by sacred prostitution — Herodotus at 1.199 on Babylon, Lucian on Byblos — has been attacked in detail by Stephanie Budin, who argues that the institution is a construct assembled by Greek writers and modern scholars rather than something the Near Eastern sources attest; her case has been influential without being universally accepted. The Kition text itself says only that certain payments were made to certain people, and it is the reader's obligation to notice how much of what is commonly said about Astarte rests on an argument still in progress."],terms:[{native:"𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕",translit:"ʿštrt",gloss:"Astarte; the most widely attested Phoenician deity in inscriptions, cognate with Mesopotamian Ishtar and the biblical Ashtoreth, and identified by Greeks with Aphrodite"},{native:"𐤀𐤔𐤌𐤍",translit:"ʾšmn",gloss:"Eshmun, the healing god of Sidon, identified with Asklepios; the etymology of the name is unsettled and every proposal is a conjecture"},{native:"𐤀𐤔𐤌𐤍𐤏𐤆𐤓",translit:"ʾšmnʿzr",gloss:"Eshmunazar, 'Eshmun has helped'; the king whose sarcophagus carries twenty-two lines, most of them a curse against opening it"},{native:"𐤕𐤁𐤍𐤕",translit:"tbnt",gloss:"Tabnit, priest of Astarte and king of Sidon, whose inscription is cut on a reused Egyptian coffin and tells the intruder there is nothing inside worth taking"},{native:"𐤑𐤃𐤍",translit:"ṣdn",gloss:"Sidon; in the Hebrew Bible and in Homer the Phoenicians are Sidonians before they are anything else"},{native:"𐤒𐤁𐤓",translit:"qbr",gloss:"grave, burial, and the act of burying; the word around which the Sidonian funerary curses turn, and the disputed word in the Pyrgi phrase 'day of the burial of the god'"}],texts:[{title:"The Eshmunazar II sarcophagus (KAI 14)",note:"Sidon, found 1855, now in the Louvre; twenty-two lines opening with a curse and recording temples built for Astarte and Eshmun and territory granted by the Persian king. Fifth century BCE by the usual dating, with a fourth-century placement argued."},{title:"The Tabnit sarcophagus (KAI 13)",note:"Sidon; inscribed on a reused Egyptian coffin. Almost the whole text is a plea not to open the burial, with a curse on anyone who does."},{title:"The Pyrgi tablets (KAI 277)",note:"Three gold sheets from the Etruscan port of Pyrgi, c. 500 BCE, excavated in 1964; a dedication to Astarte by Thefarie Velianas of Caere, and the earliest substantial bilingual for Etruscan."},{title:"The Kition tariff (CIS I 86; KAI 37)",note:"A temple account from Cyprus listing monthly payments to the personnel of Astarte's sanctuary, including the disputed klbm and grm, 'dogs' and 'puppies'."},{title:"Stephanie L. Budin, The Myth of Sacred Prostitution in Antiquity (2008)",note:"The sustained argument that the institution is a construct of Greek reporting and modern scholarship. Influential, contested, and the necessary companion to anything written about Astarte before it."}]},{slug:"votive-formula",title:"Because He Heard His Voice",native:"𐤔𐤌𐤏 𐤒𐤋",translit:"šmʿ ql",summary:"Thousands of Carthaginian stelae say almost exactly the same eleven words, and those words are the largest single body of Phoenician religious writing in existence.",paragraphs:["Of the roughly ten thousand known Phoenician and Punic inscriptions, the overwhelming majority are votive stelae, and the overwhelming majority of those come from Carthage and repeat one formula. To the Lady, to Tanit face-of-Baal, and to the Lord, to Baal Hammon: that which was vowed by so-and-so son of so-and-so son of so-and-so, because he heard his voice, may he bless him. Eleven or twelve words, with only the names varying. A modern reader arrives hoping for theology and finds a form letter. But the form is the evidence, and it discloses a specific structure of religion: a vow made under pressure, a god who hears, a thing given when the god has heard, and a blessing requested in return. The clause kšmʿ qlʾ, because he heard his voice, is the pivot; it states that the transaction has already succeeded before the stone is cut. The stele is a receipt, and the god's part was performed first.","The deities named are the Carthaginian pair. Tanit — tnt in the inscriptions, with the vocalization supplied by Latin transcriptions — appears from about the fifth century BCE onward and takes precedence over Baal Hammon in the formula, which is unusual enough in a Semitic context to have generated a literature of its own. Her epithet pn bʿl is read by most as 'face of Baal', a term for the visible presence of a god; others take pn as an element of a place-name or read the phrase as a compound divine name, and no reading has swept the field. Baal Hammon's epithet is at least as contested: ḥmn has been explained as a brazier or incense-altar, a cultic object attested in Hebrew as ḥammanim; as Mount Amanus in northern Syria; and as the town of Hammon in southern Lebanon, where the sanctuary at Umm el-Amed has produced dedications to him. All three explanations remain in print. The reader should notice that the two most frequently written divine names in the entire Phoenician corpus are both attached to epithets nobody can translate with confidence.","Tanit's sign is stranger still. On a great many stelae a figure is incised consisting of a triangle, a horizontal bar and a disc above it, sometimes with the bar's ends raised like arms. Modern scholarship calls it the sign of Tanit and disagrees about everything else: whether it is a schematic human figure, an altar, a betyl draped for a rite, a development of the Egyptian ankh, or a bottle-shaped emblem with no anthropomorphic content at all. There is no ancient text that mentions it. It appears on tens of thousands of objects and is explained by none of them — a good corrective to the assumption that a much-repeated symbol must have had a stable, statable meaning.","Beside the votives sit two texts that show the machinery of sacrifice from the priests' side. The Marseille Tariff, a stone found at Marseille in 1845 but certainly brought from Carthage, is a schedule for the temple of Baal Saphon: for each class of animal — ox, calf, ram, lamb, kid, bird — it specifies the fee due to the priests and the division of the meat, distinguishing the kll, the whole offering consumed entirely, from the ṣwʿt and the šlm kll, in which portions go to the offerer. It ends by naming the authority that issued it and prescribing penalties. A closely related tariff from Carthage itself covers the same ground. These are the only Phoenician documents that tell us in detail what a sacrifice cost, who got which cut, and who set the price; and they show a priesthood — khn, khnt, with a rb khnm at its head — functioning as an institution with published rates and an audit trail, alongside the suffetes who dated the years.","The tradition outlives its city by centuries. Carthage was destroyed in 146 BCE, and yet in Roman Africa the stelae continue: the same shapes, the same vow-and-blessing logic, now with Baal Hammon written as Saturn and Tanit as Caelestis, in Latin, into the third century CE and in places later. The Latin word for the vow, votum, occupies the slot that ndr held, and the dedicator's obligation is discharged the same way. Punic-language inscriptions in Latin letters — the Latino-Punic corpus of Tripolitania — carry the old formulae in a script the old alphabet had fathered. This is the single largest thing the Phoenician script was used for: not myth, not law, not literature, but the recording, tens of thousands of times over, that a person made a promise under difficulty and kept it afterwards."],terms:[{native:"𐤕𐤍𐤕",translit:"tnt",gloss:"Tanit, the chief goddess of Carthage, named first in the votive formula ahead of Baal Hammon; the vocalization comes from Latin transcriptions, since the script writes no vowels"},{native:"𐤐𐤍 𐤁𐤏𐤋",translit:"pn bʿl",gloss:"Tanit's standing epithet, most often rendered 'face of Baal' — the visible presence of the god; competing readings take it as a place-name element or as part of a compound divine name"},{native:"𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤇𐤌𐤍",translit:"bʿl ḥmn",gloss:"Baal Hammon; ḥmn has been explained as a brazier, as Mount Amanus, and as the town of Hammon in southern Lebanon, and all three explanations are still defended"},{native:"𐤔𐤌𐤏",translit:"šmʿ",gloss:"he heard; the verb of the clause 'because he heard his voice', which states that the god has already acted before the stone was cut"},{native:"𐤁𐤍",translit:"bn",gloss:"son; the hinge of the name formula, often repeated through three or four generations, which is why Punic prosopography is possible at all"},{native:"𐤓𐤁 𐤊𐤄𐤍𐤌",translit:"rb khnm",gloss:"chief of the priests; the head of a temple's clergy, attested in the tariffs and in dedications, and the point where the priesthood becomes visible as an institution"}],texts:[{title:"Corpus Inscriptionum Semiticarum, Pars Prima",note:"The great nineteenth- and twentieth-century edition of the Phoenician and Punic inscriptions, in which the thousands of Carthaginian votive stelae are published; the place to see how little the formula varies."},{title:"The Marseille Tariff (KAI 69; CIS I 165)",note:"A sacrificial schedule from the temple of Baal Saphon at Carthage, found at Marseille in 1845: fees, priests' portions, and the distinction between the whole offering and the shared one."},{title:"The Carthage Tariff (KAI 74; CIS I 167)",note:"A closely related and more fragmentary schedule from Carthage itself; read alongside the Marseille stone it shows the practice was regulated rather than improvised."},{title:"The Umm el-Amed inscriptions",note:"From the sanctuary in southern Lebanon that is one candidate for the Hammon of Baal Hammon's title; second century BCE, and evidence that the Carthaginian god had a Levantine home."},{title:"The Latino-Punic inscriptions of Tripolitania",note:"Punic language written in Latin characters, first to fourth centuries CE; the votive formula surviving the loss of both the city and the script."}]},{slug:"tophet",title:"The Tophet",native:"𐤌𐤋𐤊",translit:"mlk",summary:"Urns of burnt infants under votive stones, hostile classical accounts, a disputed Punic word, and a scholarly argument that has run for a century without resolution.",paragraphs:["At Salammbô on the shore below Carthage, from 1921 onwards, excavators uncovered a walled precinct in use from roughly the eighth century BCE until the city's destruction in 146, containing thousands of ceramic urns. The urns hold cremated bone: infants, in most, and in others young animals — lambs and kids — and in some, both. Many were covered by a stone stele carrying the votive formula treated in the previous current. Comparable precincts are known at Motya and Lilybaeum in Sicily, at Sulcis, Tharros, Nora and Monte Sirai in Sardinia, and at Hadrumetum in Africa. The name universally used for them, tophet, is not a Punic word and appears in no Punic inscription: it is taken from the Hebrew Bible, from the place in the valley of Ben-Hinnom outside Jerusalem where Jeremiah and the book of Kings condemn the passing of children through fire. The excavators borrowed it in the 1920s, and it has carried a conclusion inside it ever since. No ancient name for these precincts survives.","The classical accounts are lurid, specific and interested. Diodorus Siculus, at 20.14, writing about the Carthaginian crisis of 310 BCE during Agathocles' invasion, describes a bronze image of Kronos with hands sloping downward so that a child placed in them rolled into a pit of fire, and says that the Carthaginians, believing they had offended the god by substituting purchased children for their own, sacrificed five hundred — two hundred selected from the noblest families and three hundred more offered voluntarily. Plutarch, in On Superstition, describes mothers standing by without a tear while flutes and drums covered the sound. A scholion on Plato preserving Kleitarchos adds the detail of the corpse's grin. Tertullian, an African Christian writing about 197 CE, states in the Apologeticum that infants were sacrificed openly to Saturn in Africa down to the proconsulship of Tiberius, that the priests were crucified on the trees of their own temple, and that the practice continued secretly in his own day. Justin's epitome of Pompeius Trogus reports it too. Every one of these writers belonged to a culture at war with Carthage or in polemic against its religion, which does not make them wrong and does make them witnesses with a motive.","The epigraphy contributes a word rather than a verdict. Punic dedications use the term mlk in contexts where a sacrifice seems meant — mlk ʾmr, mlk bʿl, mlk ʾdm. In 1935 Otto Eissfeldt argued in Molk als Opferbegriff that mlk here is not the name of a god but a technical term for a class of offering, so that mlk ʾmr means a molk consisting of a lamb; the consequence for the Hebrew Bible, where the Masoretic Molech would then be a misread sacrificial term rather than a deity, has been disputed ever since, with John Day and George Heider arguing that Molech is a god after all. The supporting evidence is a group of Latin stelae from Ngaous in Algeria, of the second and third centuries CE, which use the word molchomor — plainly the Punic mlk ʾmr — and carry the formula a soul for a soul, blood for blood, life for life, breath for breath. Substitution is being described there in as many words. What it substitutes for is the question, and the Latin stelae are five centuries later than the Carthaginian urns.","The physical anthropology has produced the sharpest recent exchange, and it should be reported as an exchange rather than a result. In 2010 Jeffrey Schwartz, Frank Houghton, Roberto Macchiarelli and Luca Bondioli published an analysis of cremated remains from the Carthage precinct in PLoS ONE, arguing that a substantial proportion were prenatal or perinatal — too young to be plausible sacrificial victims — and that the site is better understood as a cemetery for children who died of natural causes, of whom there would have been very many. Patricia Smith, Gal Avishai, Joseph Greene and Lawrence Stager replied in Antiquity in 2011, arguing that heat-induced shrinkage in cremated bone systematically biases age estimates downward and that, corrected, the ages cluster in a narrow postnatal band consistent with sacrifice. In 2013 Paolo Xella, Josephine Quinn, Valentina Melchiorri and Peter van Dommelen published 'Phoenician bones of contention' in the same journal, arguing from the convergence of literary, epigraphic and archaeological evidence that child sacrifice took place. The earlier sceptical position associated with Sabatino Moscati and Sergio Ribichini — that the precincts were infant cemeteries and the classical accounts propaganda — retains adherents. Schwartz and colleagues have replied in turn.","Several things can be said without adjudicating. The animal urns are not disputed by anyone; whatever else the precinct is, animals were burnt and buried there under votive stones. The inscriptions themselves never state that a child was killed: they record a vow, its fulfilment, and a request for blessing, and the object of the vow is not named. The Eshmun sanctuary at Sidon, with its votive statues of children offered for healing, shows the same culture spending money on children's survival. And the practice, if it was one, does not fit the picture of an archaic survival, since the Carthaginian precinct's densest phases are in the fourth and third centuries BCE, when Carthage was at its wealthiest and most cosmopolitan. This hall does not resolve the question. It notes that the classical sources, the epigraphy and the osteology have never been made to agree, that the scholars named above are still arguing, and that a reader who has been told the matter is settled in either direction has been told something the evidence does not support."],terms:[{native:"𐤌𐤋𐤊 𐤀𐤌𐤓",translit:"mlk ʾmr",gloss:"the molk of a lamb; the Punic phrase that reappears in Latin as molchomor on the Ngaous stelae, and the strongest single piece of evidence for substitution"},{native:"𐤌𐤋𐤊 𐤁𐤏𐤋",translit:"mlk bʿl",gloss:"another molk formula, variously translated as an offering of or for a citizen, or as a molk to the lord; the rendering is unsettled"},{native:"𐤌𐤋𐤊 𐤀𐤃𐤌",translit:"mlk ʾdm",gloss:"the molk of a man, or of a commoner; the phrase around which Eissfeldt's argument and its opponents' turn, since ʾdm can be read as the offering's substance or the offerer's status"},{native:"𐤀𐤔",translit:"ʾš",gloss:"the relative particle 'that which', which introduces the vow clause on every stele; the formula says that something was vowed and never says what"},{native:"𐤌𐤑𐤁𐤕",translit:"mṣbt",gloss:"a set-up stone, a stele; the same root as nṣb, and the word by which these monuments name themselves"},{native:"𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕",translit:"qrtḥdšt",gloss:"Carthage: qrt ḥdšt, the new city; tradition dates its founding to 814 BCE from Tyre, while excavation long found nothing before the later eighth century, with radiocarbon since moving the earliest material back towards 800"}],texts:[{title:"Diodorus Siculus, Library 20.14",note:"The bronze Kronos with sloping hands and the five hundred children of 310 BCE; the most cited and most rhetorically shaped of the classical accounts, written by a Greek about a defeated enemy."},{title:"Plutarch, On Superstition 171c–d; the Kleitarchos scholion on Plato",note:"The mothers who did not weep, the flutes and drums that covered the sound, and the grin of the corpse — Greek moralising literature, valuable as evidence of what Greeks believed and reported."},{title:"Tertullian, Apologeticum 9",note:"An African writing about Africa around 197 CE: sacrifice to Saturn openly until a named proconsulship, priests crucified, and the claim that it went on secretly still."},{title:"The Ngaous stelae (Nicivibus, Algeria)",note:"Latin votive stones of the second and third centuries CE using molchomor and the formula 'a soul for a soul, blood for blood, life for life' — explicit substitution language, five centuries after Carthage fell."},{title:"The Antiquity and PLoS ONE exchange, 2010–2014",note:"Schwartz, Houghton, Macchiarelli and Bondioli for a natural-death cemetery; Smith, Avishai, Greene and Stager on cremation shrinkage and age at death; Xella, Quinn, Melchiorri and van Dommelen for sacrifice. The whole dispute is legible in these papers and their replies."}]},{slug:"lost-library",title:"The Lost Library",native:"𐤎𐤐𐤓",translit:"sēper",summary:"A Phoenician cosmogony survives only as a Greek translation of a maybe-invented priest, quoted by a Christian apologist to prove the gods were men.",paragraphs:["No work of Phoenician literature survives in Phoenician. The claim needs stating flatly because it governs everything else. There is no Phoenician myth, no hymn, no ritual manual, no law code, no chronicle in the language. The longest Phoenician text in existence is not from Phoenicia at all: it is the bilingual inscription of Azatiwada at Karatepe in Cilicia, discovered by Helmuth Bossert in 1946, some sixty lines of Phoenician set beside a Luwian version, written by a local ruler in a language that was the region's prestige medium rather than his own. The second is the Kilamuwa inscription from Zincirli, again Phoenician-language, again by a king who was not Phoenician. The literature of the coastal cities was written on papyrus and rotted; what we possess was written on stone by kings, on marble by people fulfilling vows, and on bronze by people marking arrows.","Into that vacuum comes Philo of Byblos, a Greek-writing native of the city, active roughly between 64 and 141 CE, who composed a Phoenician History in which he claimed to be translating one Sanchuniathon, a priest who lived before the Trojan War and had drawn on the writings of Taautos — that is, Thoth — and on temple records. His work survives only because Eusebius of Caesarea quoted it at length in the first book of the Preparation for the Gospel, and Eusebius quoted it for a purpose: to prove by pagan testimony that the gods of the nations were deified men. The selection is therefore shaped twice over, first by Philo's euhemerism and then by Eusebius's use of it. What comes through is a cosmogony beginning from wind and murk, a succession of divine generations, Elioun the Most High, Ouranos and Ge, and a Kronos whom Philo says the Phoenicians called El — together with the notice that El, in a time of plague and war, sacrificed his only son.","For three centuries Philo was treated as a forgery, a Hellenistic pastiche wearing an Egyptian-Phoenician costume, and Scaliger's judgement stood. The excavation of Ugarit from 1929 changed the terms of the question. The tablets from Ras Shamra revealed a Northwest Semitic mythology with El at the head of a pantheon, a storm-god Baal, Dagan, Astarte, and a structure of divine generations that Philo's Greek names could be seen to be translating rather than inventing. Otto Eissfeldt argued the case for genuine tradition behind Philo, and the modern editions — Harold Attridge and Robert Oden's translation and commentary, and Albert Baumgarten's study, both 1981 — treat him as a Hellenised transmitter of real material rather than as a fabricator. Whether Sanchuniathon ever existed remains open, and the sober position is that Philo had access to Phoenician religious tradition of some kind and presented it in a frame designed to give it antiquity. A cautionary footnote belongs here: in 1836 Friedrich Wagenfeld announced the discovery of a complete Greek Sanchuniathon in a Portuguese monastery and published it; it was a forgery, and was exposed within a year.","What Rome kept is instructive. Pliny reports at Natural History 18.22 that when Carthage fell in 146 BCE the Senate gave the city's libraries to the Numidian kings, and made one exception: the twenty-eight books of Mago on agriculture, which it ordered translated into Latin. Mago's treatise was afterwards abridged in Greek by Cassius Dionysius of Utica and quarried by Varro, Columella and Pliny himself, and it is lost except in their quotations. Of the entire written output of the greatest Phoenician city, the single work the Roman state judged worth preserving was the farming manual, and even that survives only in fragments inside other people's books. The Periplus of Hanno — a Greek text transmitted in a single medieval manuscript, claiming to render an inscription set up in the temple of Kronos at Carthage recording a voyage down the Atlantic coast of Africa — is the other survival of the same kind, and its authenticity, its date and how far south it went are all argued.","Two final witnesses show the language outliving the literature. Plautus's comedy Poenulus, written for a Roman audience around 190 BCE, opens its fifth act with the Carthaginian Hanno speaking Punic — some ten lines, followed in the manuscripts by a second version — which is, by a wide margin, the longest continuous stretch of connected Punic prose in existence. It survives because a Roman playwright needed a foreigner to be funny, and it has been worked over by every Punic scholar since Petit and Bochart in the seventeenth century. Augustine, bishop of Hippo from 396, still lived among Punic speakers: he cites Punic proverbs, complains in his letters of the shortage of clergy who can preach in the language, and records that the country people around him called themselves Chanani. And Procopius, writing in the sixth century, reports that at Tigisis in Numidia there stood columns inscribed in Phoenician letters saying: we are they who fled from before the face of Joshua the robber, son of Nun. The columns have never been found and the story is best read as what Procopius was told rather than what he saw. That is the shape of the record this hall works from — a language that lasted a thousand years past its literature, remembered in other people's books."],terms:[{native:"𐤎𐤐𐤓",translit:"spr",gloss:"a written document, and also the scribe who writes it; the word exists in the inscriptions and the thing it names does not survive"},{native:"𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤔𐤌𐤌",translit:"bʿl šmm",gloss:"Lord of Heaven; named in the Yeḥimilk inscription at Byblos and reported by Philo as Beelsamen, whom he says the Phoenicians identified with the sun — one of the few places where Philo can be checked against a Phoenician text"},{native:"𐤀𐤋",translit:"ʾl",gloss:"El, god; the head of the Northwest Semitic pantheon at Ugarit, and the deity Philo renders as Kronos — the correspondence that reopened his credibility after 1929"},{native:"𐤌𐤂𐤍",translit:"mgn",gloss:"a Punic personal name behind the Latin Mago, borne by the author of the twenty-eight books on agriculture that the Roman Senate ordered translated"},{native:"𐤇𐤍𐤀",translit:"ḥnʾ",gloss:"the Punic name Latinised as Hanno; borne by the voyager of the Periplus and by the Carthaginian who speaks Punic in Plautus"},{native:"𐤋𐤔𐤍",translit:"lšn",gloss:"tongue, language; the Punic that Augustine's parishioners still spoke four centuries after Carthage was destroyed"}],texts:[{title:"Eusebius, Preparation for the Gospel I.9–10",note:"The only substantial witness to Philo of Byblos and through him to Sanchuniathon; quoted to prove the gods were deified men, so selected for that argument and not for ours."},{title:"Harold W. Attridge and Robert A. Oden, Philo of Byblos: The Phoenician History (1981)",note:"Text, translation and commentary; with Albert Baumgarten's study of the same year, the edition in which Philo's relation to genuine Northwest Semitic tradition can be assessed."},{title:"The Karatepe bilingual (KAI 26)",note:"Cilicia, eighth century BCE, discovered 1946: some sixty lines of Phoenician beside a Luwian version. The longest Phoenician text known, and written by a ruler who was not Phoenician."},{title:"Plautus, Poenulus, Act V",note:"Hanno's Punic speech, c. 190 BCE, transmitted in two versions in the Latin manuscripts; the longest surviving connected Punic, preserved as a joke."},{title:"Pliny the Elder, Natural History 18.22–23",note:"The Senate's gift of the Carthaginian libraries to the Numidian kings and its order to translate Mago's twenty-eight books on agriculture — the only Punic book Rome deliberately kept, and lost anyway."}]}]};export{e as default};
